## Why not app-level logging instead

Wrapping `db.write()` to log directly (no trigger) is simpler but weaker for compliance: it only sees whatever SQL text/params the caller passed, can't generically produce a real before/after diff without an extra `SELECT`, and misses any write that reaches Postgres by a path other than this function (future scripts, manual fixes, a different service sharing the DB). Triggers close that gap for free since they're attached to the table, not the code path. If write volume ever makes synchronous triggers too costly, the next step up is WAL-based CDC (e.g., Debezium) reading the replication stream asynchronously — same idea, decoupled from the transaction.

**Aside, unrelated to audit design:** `PATCH /orders/:id/cancel` (`routes/orders.js:14`) doesn't check the order belongs to `req.user.accountId`, so any authenticated user can cancel any account's order. Worth fixing separately — the audit trail would at least make such cross-account writes visible after the fact, but it's not a substitute for the authorization check.

